// grpcchan.nv — a channel: one HTTP/2 connection's worth of policy,
// and the clock a deadline is measured against.
//
// Every body here is a `todo()`; the signatures are the design.
//
// A CHANNEL IS NOT A CONNECTION, AND KEEPING THEM APART IS THE POINT.
// grpc's own C implementation calls this a channel because it outlives
// the connection under it: a channel survives a GOAWAY, a reconnection
// and a change of address, and a call started on it after any of those
// is the same call from the caller's side.  So `GrpcChannel` holds the
// policy — the deadline, the metadata every call carries, the retry
// rules, the message size limits — and the transport is an argument to
// every function that performs, never a field.  A channel with a
// socket in it could not be reconnected without being rebuilt, and
// rebuilding it would lose the policy.
//
// THE DEADLINE IS WHERE THIS PACKAGE'S ONE CLOCK READING LIVES.
// grpc-codec-nv has no clock at all — `grpcmeta.remaining` takes the
// elapsed nanoseconds as a parameter — and something has to turn "five
// seconds from now" into a `grpc-timeout` header and then into "has it
// expired".  `now_nanos` is that, it is the only `[time]` row in the
// package, and every other function takes the reading as an argument
// so a deadline test is a table.
//
// AND A PROXY MUST DECREMENT THE DEADLINE.  `grpc-timeout` is a
// duration from receipt and not an instant, which is what lets a call
// cross a machine whose clock is wrong; forwarding one unchanged gives
// the next hop the whole budget again, and a chain of five proxies
// multiplies the deadline by five.  `forwarded_deadline` is that
// subtraction, published so a proxy has one function to call rather
// than arithmetic to get right.

// How this channel reaches a server.
pub struct GrpcEndpoint
    // The `:authority` pseudo-header: the host and, when it is not the
    // default, the port.  What virtual hosting and TLS verification
    // both key off.
    authority: Str
    // The `:scheme`: `https` or `http`.
    scheme: Str
    host: Str
    port: Int

// When to try a failed call again.
//
// gRPC's own retry policy, from its service config, with the names it
// uses.  It is a value here rather than JSON because a service config
// is a deployment's decision and this package's job is to apply one,
// not to parse one.
pub struct GrpcRetryPolicy
    // Including the original.  gRPC's own cap is 5; 1 turns retries
    // off.
    max_attempts: Int
    initial_backoff_ms: Int
    max_backoff_ms: Int
    // The growth ratio as a fraction, computed in integers so the same
    // policy produces the same millisecond on every target.
    multiplier_num: Int
    multiplier_den: Int
    // The statuses worth retrying.  gRPC makes this a list rather than
    // a predicate because it is a per-service decision:
    // `UNAVAILABLE` always, `RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED` only where the server
    // is known to shed load rather than to be out of quota.
    retryable_statuses: [GrpcStatus]

// Everything a channel decides once, for every call on it.
pub struct GrpcChannelOptions
    // The codec's framing limits, handed to every call.
    limits: GrpcLimits
    // Metadata every request carries: an API key, a user agent, a
    // trace header.  Per-call metadata is merged over it.
    default_metadata: GrpcMetadata
    // The deadline a call gets when it names none.  Zero means none at
    // all — which is what almost every outage postmortem blames, and
    // is the default only because a library that imposed one would
    // break the long-running streams gRPC exists for.
    default_deadline_nanos: Int
    // The compression this client offers, as a `grpc-accept-encoding`
    // value, and the one it uses for its own messages.  The codec
    // deliberately does neither: the flag says THAT a message is
    // compressed and `grpc-encoding` says with what, and the bytes are
    // the host's to inflate.
    accept_encoding: Str
    send_encoding: Str
    retry: GrpcRetryPolicy
    // How long to wait for a flow-control window to open before giving
    // up on a send.
    window_stall_timeout_ms: Int

// One channel: an endpoint, its policy, and nothing that performs.
pub struct GrpcChannel
    endpoint: GrpcEndpoint
    options: GrpcChannelOptions

// The TLS options `dial_tls` opens a session with.
//
// THE PACKAGE DECLARES IT rather than naming a standard library type.
// `compiler/stdlib/tls.nv` holds a struct of this shape, but it is not
// a published surface: there is no `docs/stdlib/tls.md`, no `use`
// brings the module into scope, and a signature naming its type
// type-checked only while the undefined-type check did not exist.  It
// does now, and it is `E2033`.
//
// AND THE NAME IS PREFIXED because struct identity is keyed by NAME
// across a whole assembly, dependencies included: two packages that
// each declared a bare `TlsConfig` could not be used by the same
// program.  docs/publishing.md, "Public type names are globally
// unique".
//
// `hostname` is both the SNI sent and the name the server's
// certificate is matched against — one field, because a program that
// could set them apart would have a way to verify the wrong name.  It
// is the endpoint's AUTHORITY and not its host when the two differ,
// which is what `tls_for` fills it from.
//
// NO ALPN FIELD.  A gRPC client that does not offer `h2` gets an
// HTTP/1.1 connection from every conforming server and fails on the
// first trailers — README rule 8 — so `dial_tls` offers
// `grpctrans.ALPN_H2` and there is no way to ask it not to.  A field
// would be a way to ask it not to.
pub struct GrpcTlsConfig
    hostname: Str
    verify_peer: Bool
    ca_bundle_path: Str

// The default retry policy: one attempt, so no retries.
//
// Off by default because a retry is a second execution, and a library
// that retried by default would be deciding that every method its
// callers write is idempotent.  gRPC's own default is the same and for
// the same reason.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${grpcchan.no_retries().max_attempts}")
// ```
pub fn no_retries() -> GrpcRetryPolicy []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.no_retries")

// gRPC's own suggested policy: five attempts, 100 ms doubling to one
// second, retrying `UNAVAILABLE` and nothing else.
//
// `UNAVAILABLE` and nothing else is `grpcstatus.is_retryable`'s answer
// too, and the agreement is deliberate.  `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED` is not
// retryable because the deadline may have passed after the work
// succeeded, so a retry does it twice; `ABORTED` is not, because the
// retry belongs at the level that knows what the transaction was.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${grpcchan.default_retries().max_attempts}")
// ```
pub fn default_retries() -> GrpcRetryPolicy []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.default_retries")

// A retry policy with the numbers spelled out.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let p = grpcchan.retry_policy(3, 100, 1000, 2, 1, [])
//     println("${p.initial_backoff_ms}")
// ```
pub fn retry_policy(max_attempts: Int, initial_ms: Int, max_ms: Int, mult_num: Int, mult_den: Int,
                    statuses: [GrpcStatus]) -> GrpcRetryPolicy []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.retry_policy")

// The backoff before attempt `attempt`, counting the first retry as 1.
//
// No jitter drawn here: gRPC's own algorithm multiplies by a random
// factor between 0.8 and 1.2, and the draw is the caller's for the
// reason it is the caller's in mqtt-nv — `[rand]` would make the one
// number nobody can see untestable, and a fleet reconnecting after a
// server restart would need an entropy source none of it has.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${grpcchan.backoff_ms(grpcchan.default_retries(), 2, 1000)}")
// ```
pub fn backoff_ms(p: GrpcRetryPolicy, attempt: Int, jitter_800_to_1200: Int) -> Int []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.backoff_ms")

// True when this status is one the policy retries.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${grpcchan.retries_status(grpcchan.default_retries(), GrpcUnavailable)}")
// ```
pub fn retries_status(p: GrpcRetryPolicy, s: GrpcStatus) -> Bool []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.retries_status")

// The default channel options: the codec's default limits, no extra
// metadata, no deadline, `identity` compression and no retries.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${grpcchan.default_options().default_deadline_nanos}")
// ```
pub fn default_options() -> GrpcChannelOptions []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.default_options")

// The endpoint a `host:port` and a scheme name.
//
// The authority is derived rather than taken, because a `:authority`
// that disagrees with the host a TLS certificate was verified for is
// the mistake this exists to prevent.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println(grpcchan.endpoint("https", "api.example.com", 443).authority)
// ```
pub fn endpoint(scheme: Str, host: Str, port: Int) -> GrpcEndpoint []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.endpoint")

// A channel over an endpoint, with the default options.
//
// Nothing is opened.  A channel is policy; the transport is an
// argument to every function that performs.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let c = grpcchan.channel(grpcchan.endpoint("https", "api.example.com", 443))
//     println(c.endpoint.host)
// ```
pub fn channel(e: GrpcEndpoint) -> GrpcChannel []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.channel")

// The options this channel applies to every call.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let c = grpcchan.channel(grpcchan.endpoint("https", "h", 443))
//     println("${grpcchan.with_options(c, grpcchan.default_options()).endpoint.port}")
// ```
pub fn with_options(c: GrpcChannel, o: GrpcChannelOptions) -> GrpcChannel []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.with_options")

// Metadata every request on this channel carries.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
// use grpcmeta
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let c = grpcchan.channel(grpcchan.endpoint("https", "h", 443))
//     println("${grpcchan.with_metadata(c, grpcmeta.empty()).endpoint.port}")
// ```
pub fn with_metadata(c: GrpcChannel, md: GrpcMetadata) -> GrpcChannel []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.with_metadata")

// The deadline a call gets when it names none, in nanoseconds.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let c = grpcchan.channel(grpcchan.endpoint("https", "h", 443))
//     println("${grpcchan.with_deadline(c, 5000000000).options.default_deadline_nanos}")
// ```
pub fn with_deadline(c: GrpcChannel, nanos: Int) -> GrpcChannel []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.with_deadline")

// The retry policy for this channel.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let c = grpcchan.channel(grpcchan.endpoint("https", "h", 443))
//     println("${grpcchan.with_retries(c, grpcchan.default_retries()).options.retry.max_attempts}")
// ```
pub fn with_retries(c: GrpcChannel, p: GrpcRetryPolicy) -> GrpcChannel []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.with_retries")

// A monotonic reading in nanoseconds.
//
// The one function in this package that reads a clock, and the only
// `[time]` row in it.  Nanoseconds and not milliseconds because
// `grpc-timeout` has a nanosecond unit and a deadline that had been
// rounded to a millisecond on the way in cannot be forwarded
// faithfully by a proxy.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io, time]
//     println("${grpcchan.now_nanos() >= 0}")
// ```
pub fn now_nanos() -> Int [time]
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.now_nanos")

// The metadata a request carries: the channel's defaults, the call's
// own merged over them, and the `grpc-timeout` the deadline becomes.
//
// One function rather than three, because the order matters and is
// invisible: a per-call `authorization` must beat the channel's, and a
// per-call deadline must beat the channel's, and a caller assembling
// the three by hand gets one of them the wrong way round.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
// use grpcmeta
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let c = grpcchan.channel(grpcchan.endpoint("https", "h", 443))
//     match grpcchan.request_metadata(c, grpcmeta.empty(), 0)
//         Ok(md) => println("${grpcmeta.count(md)}")
//         Err(_) => println("refused")
// ```
pub fn request_metadata(c: GrpcChannel, per_call: GrpcMetadata,
                        deadline_nanos: Int) -> Result<GrpcMetadata, GrpcEncodeError> []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.request_metadata")

// The instant a deadline expires, given the clock reading a call
// started at.
//
// An absolute instant inside the process and a duration on the wire:
// the two spellings exist because a machine's own clock is consistent
// with itself and two machines' clocks are not.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${grpcchan.deadline_at(1000, 5000000000)}")
// ```
pub fn deadline_at(started_nanos: Int, budget_nanos: Int) -> Int []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.deadline_at")

// True when a deadline has passed at this reading.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${grpcchan.deadline_passed(1000, 2000)}")
// ```
pub fn deadline_passed(deadline_nanos: Int, now_nanos: Int) -> Bool []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.deadline_passed")

// The deadline a PROXY forwards: what is left, not what arrived.
//
// `grpc-timeout` is a duration from receipt, so forwarding one
// unchanged gives the next hop the whole budget again and a chain of
// five proxies multiplies the deadline by five.  This is that
// subtraction, published so a proxy has a function to call rather than
// arithmetic to get right.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${grpcchan.forwarded_deadline(5000000000, 1000000000)}")
// ```
pub fn forwarded_deadline(received_nanos: Int, elapsed_nanos: Int) -> Int []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.forwarded_deadline")

// Open a TLS connection to this channel's endpoint, offering `h2` in
// ALPN.
//
// `[net]`, which is `std.tls`'s own row.  **It answers a handle and
// not a `GrpcTransport`**, because nothing in this project implements
// that trait: HTTP/2 has to be spoken over the connection this opens,
// and `grpctrans.nv` says what is missing.  The function is here so
// that the one piece the standard library CAN provide — a TLS session
// that negotiated `h2` — is written down and testable the day a
// transport exists.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println(grpctrans.ALPN_H2)
// ```
pub fn dial_tls(c: GrpcChannel, cfg: GrpcTlsConfig) -> Result<Int, GrpcTransportFault> [net]
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.dial_tls")

// A client-side TLS configuration for this channel's authority.
//
// ```novo
// use grpcchan
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let c = grpcchan.channel(grpcchan.endpoint("https", "api.example.com", 443))
//     let cfg = grpcchan.tls_for(c)
//     println(c.endpoint.authority)
// ```
pub fn tls_for(c: GrpcChannel) -> GrpcTlsConfig []
    todo("grpc-nv.grpcchan.tls_for")
